#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528025} :activity/id #uuid "36b6f9e9-f573-549e-9f71-6397aa6ad974" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc0eaaa6-3c74-5955-ab7a-40c09fa2a964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368718} {:answer/id #uuid "6b672eef-fdca-5016-828c-47d6af33c13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368719} {:answer/id #uuid "ce69df23-6261-537f-bc31-f5d9599f5543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368720} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c8552a-dd1c-59c6-825b-7bdb5b09d5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368721} {:answer/id #uuid "250a9a15-df5f-58ee-9c74-d26e624f24b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368722} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4164c5-58af-56ae-8f11-f8cd84fa0fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6decd4b-7a1d-5fb8-8bf2-963b2f6581d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73713" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo. let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "017b013a-5450-516d-883d-82ac20f6d367" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2429863-d388-592c-8258-40cbdabaf3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453306} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5526e8-026b-59b2-99ca-8ce1d8872d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453306} :answer-field/id #uuid "052e47fa-404a-580f-9ff9-15eec540dbb5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f35de4-e567-5dd3-aed6-cc95024f848d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453308} {:answer/id #uuid "d8acff99-68e0-5998-b1de-015656307d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453309} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2470bb7-9bee-5211-947d-e208a23f0783" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454404} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "767658e3-3090-5016-be61-6a9e576d338a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453310} {:answer/id #uuid "b2892fa2-c714-5afa-9d6a-a4258db3605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453311} :answer-field/id #uuid "517e811d-8c99-55a4-b8c2-34f71fd59fbf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2b565dd-1238-5428-a4d5-150ac346f6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124332" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=1-v,\\,y=u+2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=1$\n- **III.** The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-v, \\\\ u+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-v \\\\ u\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "130bb05a-f55b-5271-9ddc-91048c211b25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf5a550-ab0e-57f9-9420-6f5fc19c9793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527332} {:answer/id #uuid "544ac095-1bdb-582c-b5a1-d915b8edf13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527465} {:answer/id #uuid "7e56ec3f-c043-5d3d-9ed2-fde627fa5136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527549} {:answer/id #uuid "de661f83-ea91-5ae2-a236-5063d72239bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527748} {:answer/id #uuid "e220b7cd-3f24-57e9-9712-d0be4b086080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527754} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b8bf76-c702-59e2-a003-890dc8fb5ea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c36f01c-b274-57ce-a233-5c5a47d4ef94" :question/math-academy-id "q-274929" :question/problem "Given that $P$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, $Q$ is a $5\\times 7$ matrix, and $R$ is a $7\\times 2$ matrix, in which of the following expressions are the Hadamard products well-defined?\n\nI. $PQ⊙R$\n\nII. $PQ⊙{R}^{T}$\n\nIII. $(PQ)^{T}⊙R$" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nLet's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of ${R}^{T}$ are $2\\times 7.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $(PQ)^{T}$ are $7\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "4e2187ca-5b58-5ec6-83a8-fd8d094052e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25766927-d6f2-5fbe-a0a7-d435805ace1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.41\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527392} {:answer/id #uuid "2b023dd3-5d4c-5904-81ba-d35305307de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527404} {:answer/id #uuid "35c2b0e9-3ebd-579b-8077-e6f35b16b833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.96\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527417} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdd89a6-5722-5be8-bfb0-7a19c704530e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.14\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527717} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d733c3-af8b-53a6-abf7-8e175451a049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.40\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527717} :answer-field/id #uuid "784c4051-09fc-584b-bc9b-b914c96abf6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c60b1a3b-1eee-5254-929b-6a532b619fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143431" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two pineapples is drawn from a population. The population has pineapples of two different weights, $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $1.5\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pineapples in the population have weights $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $\\text{1.5}\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ pineapples only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 1.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =1.4\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {1.5}^{2})-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(10)-1.96 \\\\  & =2-1.96 \\\\  & =0.04\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.04}{2}=0.02\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.02}≈0.14\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "59e78201-ec48-592f-9f5a-c19cf70fbfec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b532929-031f-53ba-845b-1b69f35ae5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473278} {:answer/id #uuid "da5ae196-6ddc-5f90-8faf-ce02251fa644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8261d6df-4d7c-5438-ab24-46a9a778e5ea" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2626a4-af3b-558c-898b-e325dcd7c8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473280} {:answer/id #uuid "74014f19-619f-54ff-9fc7-8f16f269680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34c2c1-88e7-5e59-bf72-b638136d3b12" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d131820-9e7d-5679-bbf5-8ff122bec3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} {:answer/id #uuid "38a132f3-eea1-5c88-b25c-151a9517cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f148b1b-a08c-5f92-88d7-fab284af6a47" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d779a1b-dcae-59f0-822c-7860f9483ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72160" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "71f4128d-bc24-55af-9a46-51453e907aa2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7700fc74-62d9-5c43-a14e-3089802b8f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527530} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c50f8-8c07-5027-b319-7781b3d31cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527631} {:answer/id #uuid "afa06ac1-854a-5acd-a258-9948876f2387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527668} {:answer/id #uuid "d197d8cb-a53e-5fcb-9f95-86830c4ab9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527731} {:answer/id #uuid "e51a43de-aaa3-5ca0-b2ff-dd23d8bf03d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527530} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb29f16-8d05-55d0-ab1d-19d482085ecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c3f2557-93da-5d6b-97fe-29c0805f0db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75975" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "83027d2b-c1b9-55ba-91ba-820e391ce4c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fb0f21-b8a8-5312-95ea-ccce59ea666c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345578} {:answer/id #uuid "39c6a69d-17a8-5950-956f-ba6621339f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345844} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e0862-b704-5968-a5e7-6292050fbbff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346421} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5d7c18-b5de-5457-ba10-9273b03f82b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346551} {:answer/id #uuid "d48b2c7f-fcd9-5256-917c-c0d00cbdfe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347214} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ef23ce-41c7-5926-88dd-2b76f64cc792" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3bcc7f9f-485e-5418-994c-aa7e3d5166fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-79312" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "881f72c0-3118-514a-bf11-8e15d0a4f0d4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fced56e-d01f-5fd1-a5b9-535d61f4ce4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\notin (0,2),$ or $|x|\\ge 2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527339} {:answer/id #uuid "3f34cc74-45f1-5809-a233-34d9ed2b693c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\notin (0,2),$ or $|x|<2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527430} {:answer/id #uuid "4c23175b-7774-5cee-98b8-f552daf6d3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\notin (0,2),$ and $|x|<2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527455} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a4df82-563a-51ab-aef5-2059ef4e092c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\in (0,2),$ and $|x|<2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527677} {:answer/id #uuid "dca2faf0-be7b-54d7-8aa5-f4260d682846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\in (0,2),$ or $|x|<2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527430} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a43a5f5-8ea0-5a0e-95e4-95914ae5bdd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e5452f1-e9dc-544d-b364-2b179ccf8284" :question/math-academy-id "q-183347" :question/problem "$\\,$ If $x\\in (0,2),$ then $|x|<2.$\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $|x|<2.$\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x\\notin (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin (0,2),$ or $|x|<2.$"} :step/id #uuid "a6cb874c-0dc1-505f-b8a2-5ec8d9f951fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528035}}] :activity/title "Quiz 8" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527305}]}
